## Two models, one app

<At levels="simple">

So far, one model has answered every question. Many apps have two or more: a small one that is
cheap and quick, and a big one that is better and costs more. The harness decides which one
answers. One way is a <GlossaryPopover term="cascade">cascade</GlossaryPopover>: ask the small model
first, and pass the question on to the big one only when the small model seems unsure.<Cite id="chen2023frugal" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

So far, one model has answered every question. Many apps have several: a small one that is cheap
and quick, and a big one that does better (<ChapterRef to="scale" />) but costs more for every token. The harness
decides which one answers, and there are two common ways. A **router** looks at the request and
picks a model before any of them answers. A <GlossaryPopover term="cascade">cascade</GlossaryPopover>
asks the small model first and passes the question on to the big one only when the small model
seems unsure.<Cite id="chen2023frugal" />

</At>

How does a harness know the model is unsure? It can read the probabilities the model gave the
tokens it wrote (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />). Here are 40 short questions with checkable answers, put to
Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct and to Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, about three times its size.<Cite id="qwen2024" />
The small model got 26 right and the big one 37. Move the line: every question the small model
answered with less confidence than that goes to the big model instead.

<RoutingCascade />

<At levels="simple">

At 60%, the harness passes on 15 questions and gets 34 right, for 70% of the cost of always asking
the big model. At 80% it gets all 37 the big model would, and still saves a little. Confidence is a
useful hint, not proof: the small model was 79% sure that Sydney is the capital of Australia, and
only 25% sure of Ankara for Turkey, which was right.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

At 60%, the harness passes on 15 questions and gets 34 right, for 70% of the cost of always asking
the big model. At 80% it passes on 25 and gets 37, as many as the big model alone, for 95%. Every
one of the small model's 14 wrong answers had a confidence of 79% or less, but so did 11 of its 26
right ones, so a line high enough to catch every mistake also sends many good answers to the big
model. Confidence is a hint, not proof: the small model was 79% sure that Sydney is the capital of
Australia, and only 25% sure of Ankara for Turkey, which was right.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The numbers behind the slider

The confidence is the geometric mean of the probabilities $p_1, \dots, p_k$ the model gave the $k$
tokens of its answer, so a long answer isn't punished for having more tokens:

$$c = \exp\!\Big(\frac{1}{k} \sum_{i=1}^{k} \ln p_i\Big)$$

The cost counts a call as the model's number of weights: 0.49 billion for the small model and 1.54
billion for the big one, a ratio of $r \approx 3.12$. Every question pays for the small call, and
the $m$ passed on pay for a big call too, so out of $n$ questions the cascade costs

$$\frac{n + m\,r}{n\,r}$$

of asking the big model everything: 32% with $m = 0$, and 70% with $m = 15$.

</At>

Simplified: real prices are per token and set by the provider, not by the number of weights, and a
cascade adds a wait whenever it asks twice. Researchers have built cascades that matched the best
single model at a small fraction of its cost.<Cite id="chen2023frugal" /> A router avoids the second
call by deciding up front, for example with a small model trained on which questions the big model
wins; such routers have cut costs by more than half in some tests without lowering the quality of
the answers.<Cite id="ong2024" /> Either way, it's the harness, not either model, that does the
choosing.

<RecapCard>

- An app can use several models, and the harness decides which one answers.
- A router picks a model up front; a cascade asks a small model first and passes the question on
  when its confidence is low.
- The threshold trades cost against quality, and confidence is a useful hint, not proof.

<At levels="deep">

- Confidence here is the geometric mean of the answer tokens' probabilities; cost is
  $(n + m\,r)/(n\,r)$ of always asking the big model.

</At>
</RecapCard>

